Charnel-Oxide

Ingredients: FUTURE BASS & FUTURE GARAGE + GOTHIC ROCK & DEATHROCK + TRIPPY, SURREAL & PSYCHEDELIC + Woodwinds
Creative Catalyst: Reverse-engineered from an oxidised, molten cassette unearthed from a 1989 municipal time capsule in Leeds.

Charnel-Oxide sounds like a funeral procession stumbling into an illegal 4 a.m. warehouse rave: syncopated, skittering UK garage swing and gut-churning sub-bass collide with frantic, chorus-drenched deathrock basslines, while detuned future-bass chord bursts are replaced by overdriven, pitch-bent woodwinds screaming through lysergic tape delays.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 130–136 BPM. The pulse is built upon unquantized, Burial-esque 2-step garage swing layered beneath the galloping, tribal tom-tom drive of 1980s deathrock, resulting in a nervous, staggered forward lurch.
- Harmonic Palette: Dark Aeolian and Locrian minor modes disrupted by microtonal tape-wobble and Phrygian-dominant flourishes. Heavy emphasis on root-fifth drone intervals played on distorted bass guitars under microtonally sliding woodwind chords.
- Production Signatures:
  1. Gated Breath-Sidechaining: Instead of sidechaining bass to a kick drum, massive sub-bass drops are triggered and ducked by the physical breath intake of a bass clarinetist captured via throat-contact microphone.
  2. Ferro-Magnetic Seance Resynthesis (Impossible Technique): Re-recording digital masters onto discarded magnetic tape ribbons that have been literally buried in wet cemetery soil and rusted iron filings, then baking the degraded tape under infrared heat to extract non-linear harmonic decay and ghosted cross-talk from adjacent tracks.
  3. Chorus-Rake Fluting: Concert and alto flutes played with aggressive overblowing, fed concurrently through a vintage Roland JC-120 chorus amp and a granular pitch-shifter that smears single staccato notes into hallucinatory descending chords.
- Dynamics: Creeping, damp atmospherics (recorded rainfall, vinyl crackle, hollow reed clicks) that suddenly rupture into violent, wide-stereo drops where saturated 808 subs, biting post-punk guitars, and euphoric yet terrifying woodwind supersaws overwhelm the stereo field.

Scene & Origin Lore
The movement flared to life in the flooded brick basements of Batley, West Yorkshire, after urban spelunkers cracked open a welded 1989 municipal time capsule buried under a condemned shopping arcade. Inside lay a Maxell XLII cassette tape melted into a blob of iron oxide and bitumen; when audio restorers ran the mangled tape through custom heads, the audio bleed—an unholy accidental mix of an underground Batcave gothic demo, a pirate radio garage broadcast recorded over it, and catastrophic tape-flutter—spawned a sonic template that squat-party producers immediately treated as sacred scripture. The subculture, dubbed "The Spool-Heads," dresses in decayed Victorian mourning garb crossed with 1990s rave technical outerwear: rusted fishnets, rubberized anoraks lined with unspooled brown cassette ribbon, and combat boots clamped with antique clock gears. Their gatherings occur strictly in subterranean brick cisterns, where dancers huddle in heavy fog, ritualistically swinging antique metronomes while woodwind players battle drum machines in the strobe-lit gloom.

Typical Instruments: Contrabass clarinet, wooden concert flute, baroque oboe, bansuri, Rickenbacker 4001 bass (run through Electro-Harmonix Clone Theory chorus), Fender Jaguar guitar with corroded nickel strings, Roland TR-808, Akai S950 sampler, and modified 4-track cassette recorders with physical capstan drag wheels.

For Fans Of: Burial, Christian Death, Flying Lotus, Lebanon Hanover

